    "build sentences with no subject (холодно, тепло, спекотно)"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "Діалог — Ani's first November",
      "intro": "Ani had never felt temperatures below twenty degrees (Celsius) before coming to Kyiv. Andriy isn't very sympathetic. Notice that almost none of the weather sentences here has a subject.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Андрію|An-DREE-yoo], [чому|cho-MOO] [так|tak] [холодно|KHO-lod-no]?",
          "id": "Andriy, why is it so cold?",
          "literal": "Andriy (vocative), why — so — cold?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Андрій:",
          "text": "[Це|Tseh] [ще|shcheh] [не|neh] [холодно|KHO-lod-no]. [Це|Tseh] [листопад|lis-to-PAD].",
          "id": "This isn't cold yet. It's only November.",
          "literal": "this — still — not — cold. this — November."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Плюс|Plyoos] [два|dva]! [В|V] [Джакарті|dzha-KAR-tee] [завжди|ZAV-zhdi] [плюс|plyoos] [тридцять|TRID-tsyat'].",
          "id": "Two degrees! In Jakarta it's always thirty.",
          "literal": "plus — two! in — Jakarta — always — plus — thirty."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Андрій:",
          "text": "[Зачекай|Za-cheh-KAI] [до|do] [січня|SEECH-nya]. [Буде|BOO-deh] [мінус|MEE-noos] [п'ятнадцять|pyat-NAD-tsyat'].",
          "id": "Wait until January. It'll be minus fifteen.",
          "literal": "wait — until — January (genitive). will-be — minus — fifteen."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Мінус|MEE-noos] [п'ятнадцять|pyat-NAD-tsyat']? [Я|Ya] [не|neh] [виживу|VI-zhi-voo].",
          "id": "Minus fifteen? I won't survive.",
          "literal": "minus — fifteen? I — not — will-survive."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Андрій:",
          "text": "[Виживеш|VI-zhi-vesh]. [Тобі|To-BEE] [треба|TREH-ba] [тепла|TEP-la] [куртка|KOORT-ka] [і|ee] [шапка|SHAP-ka].",
          "id": "You'll survive. You need a warm jacket and a hat.",
          "literal": "will-survive. to-you — need — warm — jacket — and — hat."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[А|A] [коли|ko-LI] [буде|BOO-deh] [тепло|TEP-lo]?",
          "id": "So when will it get warm?",
          "literal": "and — when — will-be — warm?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Андрій:",
          "text": "[У|OO] [квітні|KVEET-nee]. [Весна|Ves-NA] [тут|toot] [дуже|DOO-zheh] [гарна|HAR-na].",
          "id": "In April. Spring here is really beautiful.",
          "literal": "in — April (locative). spring — here — very — beautiful."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[А|A] [літо|LEE-to]?",
          "id": "And summer?",
          "literal": "and — summer?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Андрій:",
          "text": "[Літо|LEE-to] [спекотне|speh-KOT-neh]. [Іноді|EE-no-dee] [плюс|plyoos] [тридцять|TRID-tsyat'] [п'ять|pyat'].",
          "id": "Summer is hot. Sometimes thirty-five.",
          "literal": "summer — hot. sometimes — plus — thirty — five."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Тоді|To-DEE] [я|ya] [почекаю|po-cheh-KA-yoo] [літа|LEE-ta].",
          "id": "Then I'll wait for summer.",
          "literal": "then — I — will-wait — summer (genitive)."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Андрій:",
          "text": "[Але|A-leh] [спочатку|spo-CHAT-koo] — [зима|zi-MA]. [Сьогодні|S'o-HOD-nee] [ввечері|VVEH-cheh-ree] [буде|BOO-deh] [сніг|sneeh].",
          "id": "But first comes winter. It's going to snow tonight.",
          "literal": "but — first — winter. today — in-the-evening — will-be — snow."
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**Weather sentences have no subject.** *Холодно* means \"(it's) cold\": one word, a complete sentence. There's no \"it\" or \"the weather\" to mention. ⚠️ The English habit to fight is the urge to add *it*; Ukrainian doesn't need one.",
        "**Tell холодно from холодний.** *Холодно* describes the conditions (cold all around); *холодний* is an adjective attached to a thing: *холодна вода* (cold water). Same pairs: *тепло / теплий*, *спекотно / спекотний*.",
        "**Мені холодно means \"I'm cold\".** Back to the flipped pattern from Unit 15: the condition is named, and the person goes into давальний (dative). *Мені холодно*, *Тобі жарко?*, *Йому тепло*. Compare *Тут холодно* (it's cold here), with no person at all.",
        "**Temperatures use плюс and мінус.** *Плюс два*, *мінус п'ятнадцять*. The word *градус* (degree) is almost always dropped in conversation. For zero: *нуль градусів*. Ukraine uses Celsius.",
        "**Season adverbs have their own forms, not just the season names.** *Взимку* (in winter), *навесні* (in spring), *влітку* (in summer), *восени* (in autumn). Learn all four whole: they aren't formed from the season names by a predictable pattern.",
        "**Months take у + місцевий (locative).** *У січні*, *у березні*, *у квітні*, *у травні*. The ending is **-і**, as with other nouns. To say \"until month X\", use *до* + родовий (genitive): *до січня*, *до літа*.",
        "**Ukrainian month names come from nature, not from Latin.** *Березень* from *береза* (budding birch), *квітень* from *квіти* (flowers), *листопад* literally \"leaf fall\", *грудень* from frozen clumps of earth. You can't guess them from English, but you can remember them through their pictures."
      ],
